  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 402 AM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\n- Thunderstorms this afternoon and tonight with heavy rain\n\nHigh pressure is in place this morning bringing quiet and dry\nweather to the Great Lakes region. Upstream showers and \nthunderstorms have formed over portions of Minnesota. The\nexpectation is that these storms will congeal and move our\ndirection through the course of the morning. These showers and\nstorms should move into the area this afternoon, after 200pm.\nFeel there is more of a heavy rain threat this afternoon and\ntonight than a severe threat, but cannot rule out severe weather\neither. Machine learning schemes show all severe weather hazards \nin play today as a frontal boundary will be draped across our \nsouthern forecast area near I-94. Wind looks to be the primary \nhazard near and south of I-96. MUCAPE values will be in the \n1000-2000 j/kg range with deep layer shear on the order of 30-40 \nknots. We will be monitoring the state of the atmosphere as the \nactivity moves in during the afternoon. There is enough of a \nmoisture surge that comes in though which should keep instability \nare a moderate level. PWAT values rise from near 1.00 inch to \nstart the day, surging towards 1.7 inches late in the day. A surge\nof that magnitude in the mid summer can bring both severe weather\nand hydro/flooding concerns. We are in a day 1 slight risk for \nboth severe weather and flooding potential. The HREF continues to \nshow some rather impressive 48 hour max rainfall totals. It is \nindicating the potential for some rainfall amounts that may \nlocally push towards 5 inches through the day on Monday.\nModels continue to show redevelopment/training potential into this\nevening and tonight at the low level jet provides steady lift over\na cold pool that will be continually reinforced. Short term\nflooding headlines are very possible this afternoon and evening.\n\n- Chances for showers/storms Mon-Tue but focus may be south\n\nChances for showers and storms continue into Monday and Tuesday,\nbut the potential does not look as good as it did yesterday. The\nfocus of the low level jet is more off to our south, but the GFS\ndoes bring it back in on Tuesday. There is model disparity with\nregard to the LLJ in this time frame so confidence in forecast\nparticulars is not high. Expecting at least some showers and\nstorms in the south on the northern periphery of thunderstorm\ncomplexes rolling from IL to IN/OH. Any additional rainfall during\nthis time could add to issues associated with what falls today.\nThe day 2 (Monday) slight risk is generally south of our area and\ncannot argue with that given that is where the LLJ is focused.\n\n- Drying out mid to late week\n\nSurface high pressure builds in for mid to late week which should\nbring a respite from the active weather of earlier in the week. We\nactually look to dip back into the 70s for highs Thursday, Friday\nand Saturday.",
